(
Implenting does> which is immediate. It generates a call to dodoes which is 
not immediate, followed by a "ret". The dodoes creates code, assuming a single
stack value, perhaps a pointer, which it converts to code. It then generates
code for a "jmp" to the code following the "does>"

Finally it updates the topmost word dictionary-entry so it points to the code
above.

The value that the code following does> finds on the stack must be topmost
on the stack when calling does>
)

: dodoes (data --)
	comp:init
	
	comp.next       (data code-start)
	0 c>C           (--write dummy length byte--)
	
	swap            (code-start data)
	
	(-- write data pointer as code --)
	OP_CVAL c>C
	>C
	
	(-- jump to code following does> --)
	
	OP_CVAL c>C
	R@ 1+ >C
	OP_JMP c>C
	
	comp:done
	
	dup comp.next swap - 1- (code-start length) (1- to skip past length byte)
	(-- write length byte --)
	over c!               (code-start)
	
	1+                    (actual-code-start)  (-- after length byte now at index -1 --)
	
	(-- update top dictionary word address --)
	*dictHead DE->address !
	DE_NORMAL *dictHead DE->type c!
;

: does>
	(build call to dodoes, then add OP_RET - the code following does> comes after that OP_RET)
	' dodoes ADDR_CALL_BIT | >C
	OP_RET c>C
; immediate

